    <DataNode elementId="LOC_OS05G0482400-MONOMER" textLabel="gibberellin 16α, 17-epoxidase" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q5KQH7" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="600" centerY="395" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="THE-ARABIDOPSIS-INFORMATION-RESOURCE, dreher">This CYP714D1 cytochrome P450 enzyme was first discovered based on the gibberellin-related growth phenotype observed in the recessive &lt;i&gt;elongated uppermost internode (eui)&lt;/i&gt; mutant |CITS: [16399803]|. As suggested by the mutant name, the uppermost internode of the &lt;i&gt;eui&lt;/i&gt; mutant is extremely elongated, which is a beneficial phenotype in the generation of male sterile cultivars used to produce hybrid rice seed |CITS: [16399803]|. &lt;i&gt;eui&lt;/i&gt; mutants also have 24-fold higher levels of the bioactive |FRAME: CPD1F-139 gibberellin A1| and much higher levels of |FRAME: CPD1F-137 gibberellin A4| than wild type plants |CITS: [16399803]|. This phenotype can be explained by the biochemical activty of this enzyme as a gibberellin 16&amp;alpha;, 17-epoxidase that inactivates gibberellins. Assays performed using microsomal fractions of yeast expressing this enzyme indicate that CYP714D1 catalyzes the formation of epoxides using GA4, GA9, and GA12, all of which have a hydrogen instead of a hydroxy group at position 13 (13-H) |CITS: [16399803]|. Very little activity was detected with GA1, GA20, and GA53, three hydroxylated (13-OH) gibberellins. &lt;i&gt;In planta&lt;/i&gt; analyses support the hypothesis that 13-H GAs are the preferred substrates of this enzyme because rice plants over-expressing CYP71D1 respond more strongly to treatment with GA1 (13-OH) than GA4 or GA9 (13-H), presumably because these plants are better able to inactivate the latter |CITS: [16399803]|. Although the exact biochemical reactions carried out by this enzyme have not been identified to date, based on comparisons with other cytochrome P450 enzymes, it is likely that NAD(P)H and O&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; are involved in this reaction. The expression pattern of the EUI transcript is consistent with the phenotype observed in &lt;i&gt;eui&lt;/i&gt; mutants. For instance, &lt;i&gt;EUI&lt;/i&gt; and &lt;i&gt;Eui-GUS&lt;/i&gt; expression appear to be high in the lowest portion of the uppermost internode |CITS: [16399803]|. Related proteins can be idenitified in a number of plant species. The most closely related homolog in Arabidopsis (&lt;A href=&quot;http://www.arabidopsis.org/servlets/TairObject?type=locus&amp;name=AT5G24910&quot;&gt;CYP741A1&lt;/A&gt;) does not seem to posses the same enzymatic activity based on the microsomal assay system used to identify the activity of CYP714D1|CITS: [16399803]|. But it is possible that other plant species may have proteins that function as gibberellin epoxidases, especially given the fact that putative downstream products of this enzyme have been identified in many plant species (see references in |CITS: [16399803]|)).</Comment>
